---
source: compiler/null_check/tests/diagnostics.rs
expression: out
---
--- object @o "O" { location [0m 0m 1deg] }\n
case.noll:1:34 error[null-unit]: `location` cannot take `deg`: it takes m, cm, mm, km, um, nm, pm, mi, yd, ft, in, thou or a bare number
--- camera @c "C" { clip-start 1s }\n
case.noll:1:29 error[null-unit]: `clip-start` cannot take `s`: it takes m, cm, mm, km, um, nm, pm, mi, yd, ft, in, thou or a bare number
--- camera @c "C" { lens 5zz }\n
case.noll:1:23 error[null-unit]: `zz` is not a unit `lens` takes; it takes mm, cm, m, um or a bare number
  fix: write `mm`
  `write `mm`` gives: camera @c "C" { lens 5mm }\n
--- object @d "D"\nlight @l "L" {\n  type point\n  energy driver="spin * 100Q" {\n    var spin transforms { target @d transform-type=rot-z }\n  }\n}\n
case.noll:4:28 error[null-unit]: `Q` is not a unit, after the number `100`
--- object @d "D"\nlight @l "L" {\n  type point\n  energy driver="bpy.context.scene.frame_current * 1W" {\n    var spin transforms { target @d transform-type=rot-z }\n  }\n}\n
case.noll:4:52 error[null-unit]: `1W` has a unit, which Null compiles only in Blender's simple expressions; this one is Python (`.` is not in Blender's simple expressions), so write its numbers as Python does
